//! fuse.rs — the libfuse plugin as ONE file.
//!
//! GENERATED by `gen_fuse.py` — do not hand-edit. The multi-file layout
//! under `src/` is the build intermediate; this file is the deliverable.
//! Regenerate with `python3 gen_fuse.py` (it re-runs gen_kernel.py and
//! gen_methods.py first, then assembles this file). Re-running produces
//! the identical file byte-for-byte.
//!
//! Strictly-typed Rust schema for the libfuse C library (3.19, kernel
//! protocol 7.46).
//!
//! The types in this one file are generated from libfuse's public headers
//! (`include/*.h`) and use concrete Rust types throughout — every
//! schema type has a typed schema, and nothing untyped is smuggled
//! in. C structs become Rust structs with exact integer widths; C
//! callback tables become Rust traits ([`FuseOperations`],
//! [`FuseLowlevelOps`], [`CuseLowlevelOps`]) whose methods return
//! `Result<_, FuseError>`; the kernel wire protocol is the `Fuse*`
//! record set below.
//!
//! # The plugin pattern
//!
//! This file follows the opdbus plugin shape: [`FuseState`] is the
//! single typed present-state contract, every
//! dispatchable operation has a typed Input/Output pair,
//! [`dispatch_fuse_method`] and
//! [`dispatch_fuse_lowlevel_method`] route incoming kernel operation
//! calls to a filesystem implementation, and [`fuse_schema()`]
//! builds the complete schema document — state + methods +
//! capabilities + signals — as one sealed unit (`schemas/plugin.json`).
//! The direct `impl StatePlugin` wiring for his `op_state` /
//! `op_state_store` crates drops into the marked section at the
//! bottom of this file; those crates are not available here.
//!
//! Direction: libfuse is a library you *implement* (callbacks), not
//! a service you call — dispatch routes *incoming* operation calls
//! to the trait implementation, the reverse of a service plugin that
//! calls out. There is no acknowledgement step: a method call returns
//! its typed Output directly, and that IS the response.
//!
//! # Fail closed
//!
//! The schema is the single source of truth and the code matches it
//! exactly. Unknown fields are rejected (`deny_unknown_fields` on
//! every Input type, the state, and the schema document); malformed
//! input fails instead of becoming an empty/default value; there is
//! no silent coercion that bends data into shape. If it doesn't fit
//! the schema exactly, it's an error, not a best-effort parse.
//!
//! # `serde_json::Value` at the dispatch boundary only
//!
//! `serde_json::Value` appears in this file in exactly one place:
//! the dispatch boundary — [`typed_input`] and the two
//! `dispatch_*` functions, which deserialize the JSON envelope into
//! the strict Input type, exactly as in his emqx.rs. No schema type
//! field is a `Value`; every field has a concrete typed schema.
//! Anything that cannot be given a typed schema is left out entirely
//! (it falls out of the design) — see the crate README for the list.
//!
//! Every public schema type also derives `schemars::JsonSchema`, and
//! the single sealed schema document is `schemas/plugin.json` —
//! state + methods + capabilities + signals in one file, ready to
//! seal into a blob. (Per-type JSON files under `schemas/` are
//! inspection intermediates, not the deliverable.) Regenerate them
//! with `cargo run --bin gen_schemas`. Each file is self-contained
//! (subschemas inlined, draft 2020-12) and deterministic
//! (byte-for-byte reproducible from the same input, no timestamps,
//! no live data). The types are the single source of truth: the
//! schema and the typed operation traits both derive from them.
//! Generation is a pure derivation from the C headers — no cache, no
//! mirrors, no cached intermediate state — and the runtime code
//! holds no cached state either.
//!
//! # Scope note
//!
//! This file is the schema/type layer only: it does not link
//! libfuse and cannot mount a filesystem. The session and mount
//! entry points are modelled as the [`FuseApi`] trait
//! (typed signatures, defaulting to [`FuseError::NotLinked`]); an
//! FFI backend that links libfuse implements it.
//!
//! # Quick start
//!
//! ```no_run
//! use libfuse_rs::{FuseConnInfo, FuseFileInfo};
//! use libfuse_rs::{FuseError, FuseLowlevelOps, FuseReq, LowlevelReply};
//!
//! struct MyFs;
//!
//! impl FuseLowlevelOps for MyFs {
//!     fn getattr(
//!         &mut self,
//!         req: FuseReq,
//!         ino: libfuse_rs::FuseIno,
//!         fi: Option<&mut FuseFileInfo>,
//!     ) -> Result<LowlevelReply, FuseError> {
//!         let _ = (req, ino, fi);
//!         Err(FuseError::NotImplemented)
//!     }
//! }
//! ```

